WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.520
Canada closed the cod fishery to protect newfoundland stocks, then steered fishes towards Snowcrab, only

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:10.460
to create a new single species dependence that proved just as fragile.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.920
This is unintended consequences, episode 113.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.820
In the spring of 1992, the federal government announced that the northern cod fishery off

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.720
Newfoundland and Labrador would close indefinitely.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.440
The decision aimed to give the depleted stocks time to recover after decades of heavy harvesting.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.360
Instead, the policy that followed pushed many of the same fishing communities into Snowcrab,

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:40.680
setting the stage for a later economic shock when crab landings also began to fall.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.640
Federal fisheries managers faced a stark reality after the cod collapse.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:50.420
Thousands of inshore and near-shore fishers suddenly had no legal harvest.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:56.160
Officials believed that redirecting effort toward underutilized species such as Snowcrab

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.460
could keep boats working and preserve the social fabric of coastal towns.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.560
At the time, crab populations appeared healthy and market prices were strong, making the shift

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:12.520
seem like a practical bridge to a more diversified fishery. The approach reflected the prevailing view

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:17.480
that temporary effort redirection, paired with license adjustments and vessel support,

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:23.700
could stabilize incomes while cod rebuilt. Decision makers operated with the data then available,

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:29.720
which showed crab biomass well above historical lows and no immediate signs of the environmental

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:35.160
changes that would later emerge. Their goal was straightforward. They wanted to prevent total

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:41.080
economic collapse in the short term by giving fishers an alternative within the same marine ecosystem.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:47.300
Most vessels lacked the gear, deck space, and crew training to switch among several targets at once.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.760
A single alternative allowed quicker re-equipment and kept the existing fleet size intact.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:58.720
Managers also weighed the political reality that abrupt fleet reductions would have required far

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:04.460
larger compensation packages than the federal budget could support at the time. Beginning in the

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:10.500
middle of the 1990s, fisheries and oceans Canada expanded Snowcrab quotas and simplified entry

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:16.100
rules for vessels that had previously targeted cod. License buyouts and temporary permits

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:23.680
encourage fishers to re-gear their boats for crab traps rather than leave the industry. Early

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:28.820
communities reported renewed activity at warves that had gone quiet after the moratorium.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:34.680
Proponents pointed to rising export revenues and stable crew employment as evidence that the

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:40.300
substitution was working. A few biologists noted that concentrated harvest on a single species

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:45.760
carried risks if environmental conditions shifted, yet the immediate priority remained keeping

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:52.100
families on the water. By the early 2000s, the pattern was well established. The same ports that

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:58.040
once processed cod now depended on crab deliveries. Skeptics wondered whether the new quotas would

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:03.980
simply repeat the overcapacity problem that had affected cod. In practice, the department tied new

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:09.420
crab allocations to vessel length and historical participation, which limited the number of new

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:15.480
entrants and appeared to spread effort more evenly than the open access cod era. Processing plants

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:21.660
converted their lines from flaying cod to sectioning and freezing crab, an investment that locked capital

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:27.260
into the new species, but also created short-term jobs in communities that had seen plants close.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:33.780
The policy therefore succeeded on its own narrow terms. Boats stayed active, exports statistics

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:40.480
improved, and the visible signs of economic distress receded for roughly a decade. As the 2010s

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:45.900
progressed, snow crabs stocks declined sharply in several key areas around Newfoundland and Labrador.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.780
Warming ocean temperatures altered the crab's habitat and reproductive cycles,

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:56.180
while the concentrated fishing effort that had built up after the cod closure removed larger,

00:03:56.380 --> 00:04:02.140
older individuals at higher rates. Because many enterprises had invested heavily in crab-specific

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:08.200
gear and quota, they lacked the flexibility to pivot again when landings dropped. The result was

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:13.180
a second wave of economic pressure on the same coastal communities the original policy had sought

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:19.700
to protect. Processing plants that had retooled for crab faced reduced throughput and some smaller

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:25.980
operators sold quota or left the fishery altogether. The substitution had replaced one narrow dependence

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:32.140
with another rather than spreading risk across multiple species. Second order effects included

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:38.060
increased debt loads tied to crab vessels and a loss of traditional ecological knowledge associated

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:44.440
with mixed species fishing. Third order effects appeared in municipal budgets and social services as

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:50.460
seasonal unemployment rose once more. The causal chain ran directly from the well-intentioned effort

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:55.780
to maintain fishing livelihoods through a single alternative stock whose own vulnerabilities had

00:04:55.780 --> 00:05:03.060
not been fully anticipated. The capital already committed specialized traps, larger winches, refrigerated

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:09.200
holds size for crab, raised the cost of switching to other species beyond what most small operators

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:15.580
could finance without new loans. In addition, the quota system itself rewarded consistent participation

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:22.940
in the crab fishery, so leaving even temporarily risk losing future allocations. These feedback loops

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:29.040
meant that the very mechanisms designed to stabilize incomes after cod also reduced the system's

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:34.980
ability to absorb the next shock. By the late 2010s, federal and provincial governments began

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:40.500
exploring quota reductions and area closures for snow crab in response to stock assessments.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:46.400
Some fishers received temporary support payments while others pursued limited diversification into

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:52.660
species such as shrimp or lump fish. The experience prompted renewed discussion of ecosystem-based

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:58.220
management that considers multiple stocks and climate trends together, yet progress has been

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:04.640
gradual. No simple reversal of the earlier policy was possible. The capital and licenses already

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:11.280
committed to crab made a quick return to broader harvesting difficult. Today, many newfoundland communities

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:16.600
continue to navigate the narrow margin between crab availability and economic viability,

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:23.380
illustrating how one crisis-driven adjustment can embed new fragilities. When a sudden shock prompts

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:28.820
rapid redirection toward a single alternative resource, the new activity can inherit the same

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:35.160
concentration risks that undermine the original system. Building genuine resilience requires deliberate

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:41.200
attention to ecological variability and the distribution of effort across several options rather than a

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:47.960
one-to-one swap. Incentive structures that reward immediate economic continuity can unintentionally

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:54.200
lock participants into the next vulnerable stock. The newfoundland case invites us to ask whenever

00:06:54.200 --> 00:07:00.240
we design a substitute in response to crisis, whether we are spreading risk or simply moving the same

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:05.660
narrow dependence to a different part of the ecosystem. And that's where today's story leaves us.

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:11.680
If it changed how you see one everyday decision, that's the whole point. Pass it along to someone

